\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around three analyses: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type partial ordering (C2, including the inconclusive layer effect C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 are primary claims; C4 is exploratory.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, conditional OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity (\S\ref{sec:method}). A Type III sum-of-squares decomposition\footnote{Type III SS avoids ordering dependence in our unbalanced design; logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant results (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).} partitions variance as: clause identity 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), model 1.0\%, condition 15.8\%, residual 60.8\% (of which $\sim$15\% is stochastic and $\sim$46\% systematic, estimated from an OpenAI-family ICC replication; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). A permutation test confirms the clause effect exceeds chance ($p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_variance}). Individual clause wording---not type membership---drives clause-level variance. The incompleteness effect ($+$49.2\,pp) is an upper bound that includes information addition; controlling for token count changes no type's predicted effect by more than 0.1\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). The confound-free range is $+$18.7\,pp (coreferential) to $+$36.0\,pp (authorization scope); excluding incompleteness entirely, the pooled effect is $+$31.0\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$).

Replication on two additional model families confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); SOPBench~\citep{li_sopbench_2025} suggests directional consistency ($+12.6$\,pp mean; three of five significant; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). For cross-benchmark replication, directional consistency across all models is the primary criterion; effect-size calibration requires grain-matched measurement (Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). The taxonomy's value lies in predicting failure mechanisms an order of magnitude more strongly than violation rate (NMI $= 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_variance_decomposition.pdf}
\caption{Variance decomposition of violation outcomes (Type III SS). Clause wording (22.4\%) is the largest actionable component; type membership explains only 0.4\%. The dashed line separates actionable factors (39.2\%) from residual variance (60.8\%, of which $\sim$15\% is stochastic and $\sim$46\% systematic, OpenAI-family estimate; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}).}
\label{fig:variance_decomposition}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Type Partial Ordering (C2)}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Violation rates (\%) under ambiguous policy clauses by type and model (5.4 = GPT-5.4, 4.1 = GPT-4.1, Cla.\ = Claude, Qw3 = Qwen3, DS = DeepSeek). $\Delta$pp: pooled difference (ambiguous $-$ unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. Tier: TOST equivalence cluster at $\pm 15$\,pp---U = upper bound (separated at all margins), M = mid-tier (6/6 pairwise equivalent), L = lower (separates at $\pm 12$\,pp). All per-type $p < 10^{-5}$ (Fisher's exact).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.8pt}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr@{\hskip 6pt}cr}
\toprule
Layer & Type & Pooled & 5.4 & 4.1 & Cla. & Qw3 & DS & $\Delta$pp\,[95\% CI] & Tier & $p$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{speccolor}{Spec.}} & Incompleteness$^\dagger$ & 53.6 & 46 & 58 & 48 & 62 & 54 & +49.2\,[42.2, 55.6] & U & $<\!10^{-37}$ \\
 & Auth.\ Scope$^\ddagger$ & 41.6 & 64 & 46 & 12 & 26 & 60 & +36.0\,[29.1, 42.6] & M & $<\!10^{-22}$ \\
 & Cond.\ Prec. & 36.8 & 34 & 36 & 36 & 40 & 38 & +30.8\,[24.0, 37.4] & M & $<\!10^{-17}$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{lingcolor}{Ling.}} & Lexical & 42.8 & 42 & 34 & 32 & 52 & 54 & +35.2\,[28.0, 42.0] & M & $<\!10^{-20}$ \\
 & Scopal & 39.4 & 42 & 38 & 32 & 37 & 48 & +34.2\,[27.4, 40.7] & M & $<\!10^{-21}$ \\
 & Coreferential & 31.6 & 18 & 32 & 22 & 42 & 44 & +18.7\,[11.5, 25.7] & L & $<\!10^{-6}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textbf{Overall}} & \textbf{41.0} & 41 & 41 & 30 & 43 & 50 & \textbf{+34.0}\,[31.3, 36.9] & --- & $<\!10^{-105}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize $\dagger$\,Subject to information-addition confound; see \S\ref{sec:results} and Appendix~\ref{app:confound}. $\ddagger$\,Interpret with caution: lowest inter-judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$); see measurement caveats in text. Excluding incompleteness: pooled $\Delta = +31.0$\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$).}
\end{table*}

The six types are not interchangeable. An omnibus $\chi^2$ test on ambiguous-condition violation rates rejects the uniformity null ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $df = 5$, $p = 3.6 \times 10^{-5}$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$). TOST equivalence testing at a $\pm 15$ pp margin reveals a graded equivalence structure among five types---authorization scope (41.6\%), lexical (42.8\%), scopal (39.4\%), and conditional precedence (36.8\%) form a mid-tier cluster, with coreferential (31.6\%) below---while incompleteness (53.6\%) stands apart as an upper bound---conditional on the confound controls in Appendix~\ref{app:confound}---whose ranking partly reflects an information-addition confound. Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} present the full breakdown.

Sensitivity analysis ($\pm 5$--$20$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:tost}) and bootstrap clustering ($B = 10{,}000$; Appendix~\ref{app:cluster}) confirm stable extremes across all margins; mid-tier cohesion emerges at $\pm 12$\,pp. Cross-model concordance is moderate ($W = 0.398$, $p = 0.072$; Appendix~\ref{app:kendall_w}).

An important qualification: a three-family cross-judge validation yields concordant type rankings ($\rho = 0.71$--$0.89$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_judge}), but two-group rankings are discordant ($\rho = 0.09$, $p = 0.87$; Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}), driven entirely by authorization scope$^\ddagger$. As a sensitivity check, excluding authorization scope yields $\rho = 0.90$ ($p = 0.037$); the remaining five types preserve the same partial ordering (incompleteness as upper bound, coreferential as lower bound, with three mid-tier types equivalent at $\pm 15$\,pp). C2 establishes robust extremes---not a complete linear ordering.

Incompleteness is highest in four of five models ($\geq$46\% in all seven; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); its unambiguous variant adds policy content, so the 49.2\,pp effect is an upper bound (confound analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). Coreferential provides a confound-free lower bound at 31.6\%. The two-layer grouping (C3) did not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction ($p = 0.017$, adjusted threshold $0.0125$) and serves as organizational convenience rather than an empirical claim.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. Types form a partial ordering: incompleteness as upper bound (conditional on information-addition confound), coreferential as lower bound, and a mid-range equivalence cluster. Mid-tier ordering is margin-dependent (\S\ref{sec:results}).}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Authorization scope varies 52\,pp across models ($\kappa = 0.385$; interpret with caution); incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%). C4 interaction is authorization-scope-specific; see measurement caveats in text.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

\label{sec:c4}C4 (model$\times$type interaction) is exploratory. A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$), with Claude (30.3\%) least vulnerable and DeepSeek (49.7\%) most (cross-judge validated; Fleiss' $\kappa = 0.717$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_judge}). The interaction is driven entirely by authorization scope---the type with lowest inter-judge reliability ($\kappa = 0.385$), near-zero within-type ICC, and the sole driver of cross-judge ranking inconsistency ($\rho = 0.09$; \S\ref{sec:results})---raising the possibility that C4 reflects measurement instability rather than genuine model$\times$type interaction (Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap}). Excluding authorization scope, the interaction vanishes ($p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}). We therefore treat this finding as suggestive only.

